{"body": "The past tense agrees with the subject's gender: 'byłem' (m.), 'byłam' (f.), 'byliśmy' / 'byłyśmy' in the plural.", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "ec8f787ab3a4fe6e00c5c05236c2c1d7c0e4e848ca6d8bc8f5e76dda4f36bab0", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.czas-przeszly", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Past tense and gender endings"}
{"body": "The imperfective future is 'będę' plus the infinitive or the past participle: 'będę czytać' or 'będę czytał'.", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "95d06bdb7bbe655403da18192c3e8208c17ffdbdbf0b93a8e122d9e81733c985", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.czas-przyszly", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Compound future with będę"}
{"body": "The imperative is common in requests: 'Napisz mi.' Politeness usually comes from 'proszę' or a question form rather than from avoiding the imperative.", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "a72b1bf72ee8aaae1511922d9603c0e5136a785f672aec752002db3da6e8f677", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.tryb-rozkazujacy", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Imperative for requests"}
{"body": "'się' is a separate clitic and never carries stress: 'Jak się nazywasz?', 'Nazywam się Anna.'", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "cc21e71b7eefc2e7c3840069463ee4cb966dab4835fce4f844b1233842e11561", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.sie", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Placement of reflexive się"}
{"body": "Regular comparatives add '-szy' or '-ejszy': 'tani' becomes 'tańszy'. 'dobry' is irregular: 'lepszy'.", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "c520d7f70b9f8ac74e9fc1f377911b890aea4b0d64d1b66249d25a0857e85b69", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.stopniowanie", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Comparative of adjectives"}
{"body": "After 2, 3, 4 a masculine inanimate or neuter noun stays in the nominative plural: 'dwa bilety'. From 5 upward the noun takes the genitive plural: 'pięć biletów'.", "kind": "grammar", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "92c1b298778361b00b027cfd1e35c8fb2c7eca6501365b9bda264056054854e7", "dependencies": [{"kind": "content", "on_change": "needs-review", "reference": "pl.lex.bilet"}], "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.gram.liczebniki", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Numerals with nouns: 2-4 against 5 and above"}
{"body": "Modal verbs take a bare infinitive: 'Muszę iść', 'Mogę zapłacić', 'Chcę zamówić'.", "kind": "construction", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "8333c8ffc1403c585b1b2092a3c9ea5ccc741bf10231c75d408edfd35fed5224", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.constr.modalne", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "muszę, mogę, chcę plus infinitive"}
{"body": "'Czy mogę ...?' asks permission politely: 'Czy mogę zapłacić kartą?' Dropping 'czy' is normal in speech.", "kind": "construction", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "778177df2d5c2ce459ae5cae278113c925ea98f3997f99beba6d4029c4fa5ea1", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.constr.czy-moge", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Czy mogę ...? as a polite request"}
{"body": "Complaints of pain use 'boli mnie' with the accusative: 'Boli mnie głowa', 'Bolą mnie zęby' in the plural.", "kind": "construction", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "349f612d873b08fcbe6cdeae04a1d8fff3d2d8d4a956ef09d97c7a3dd9d40a1f", "dependencies": [{"kind": "content", "on_change": "needs-review", "reference": "pl.lex.bol"}], "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.constr.boli-mnie", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Boli mnie plus the body part"}
{"body": "Ask a price with 'Ile to kosztuje?' or 'Ile płacę?'. The answer uses 'złoty' with the numeral rules: 'dwa złote', 'pięć złotych'.", "kind": "construction", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "1bc72484e204492a631e6d2282a384a8a40e497ce6a6644cc27328463a064318", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.constr.ile-kosztuje", "themes": ["podróże"], "title": "Ile to kosztuje? for prices"}
{"body": "'ą' and 'ę' are nasal: 'wąż', 'gęś'. Word-final 'ę' is usually de-nasalised in speech: 'proszę' sounds like [prɔʃɛ].", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "9d400761ca1058dfa8dda4b10b3ee055f879c8e3100456190e5af0937d527d22", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.nosowki", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Nasal vowels ą and ę"}
{"body": "'sz' is retroflex and 'ś' is palatal: 'kasza' against 'Kasia'. Confusing them changes the word.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "86398e44ae5b93f7c2d9c05788836c46b492c6fe9cffcf109f304ea7886bf6ec", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.sz-s", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Contrast sz against ś"}
{"body": "'cz' is retroflex, 'ć' palatal: 'czy' against 'ci'. 'ci' before a vowel spells the same sound as 'ć'.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "748dbffd56931a4fac1a391b06bc7a528988abaf0755f4ca2c3631dfea44682d", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.cz-c", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Contrast cz against ć"}
{"body": "'dz' as in 'dzwon', 'dź' as in 'dźwig', 'dż' as in 'dżem'. They differ in place of articulation, not in voicing.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "8c8474d1bcb89fdc4cf68b48ed5393b3df44627759fc6abf2275870df4788ab9", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.dz-series", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "The dz, dź and dż series"}
{"body": "'rz' and 'ż' are pronounced identically; the spelling follows etymology: 'rzeka' against 'żaba'.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "5b009c965146ab3040e11042443df42a886430faacea99fe5793087c1fc19835", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.rz-z", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "rz and ż spell one sound"}
{"body": "'ó' and 'u' are both [u]: 'mój' rhymes with 'kluj'. 'ó' usually alternates with 'o' in related forms.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "e563f044a8fe7c38d1ba1f5226458916b58e608f5eea66bfbab7251f4c5617b2", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.o-u", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "ó and u spell one sound"}
{"body": "In standard Polish 'ch' and 'h' are both [x]: 'chleb', 'herbata'.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "679978a9fcfecae0007823b9c34bf08ea33895e5b20a8af9def0546fd083c042", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.ch-h", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "ch and h spell one sound"}
{"body": "'ł' is a [w] glide, not an l: 'łódka' begins like English 'wood'.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "803a430be31281244fe8c9a07c2b72d317753e1a347fc3063736c9d242db6d2e", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.l-kreska", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "The letter ł"}
{"body": "Polish stresses the second-to-last syllable: 'ko-BIE-ta', 'te-LE-fon'. A few borrowings and verb forms are exceptions.", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "fb47d0618d560f2fb23cd02016413defcae1aca8608538117654d035db47d302", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.akcent", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Stress on the penultimate syllable"}
{"body": "Voiced obstruents devoice at the end of a word: 'chleb' sounds like [xlɛp], 'róg' like [ruk].", "kind": "pronunciation", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "c17dbeb852c61eb8a2dfdd2e7404692ef5cba9b12d28f5f4c1d3de6ce24480bb", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.pron.wyglos", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Final devoicing"}
{"body": "Polish adds ą, ć, ę, ł, ń, ó, ś, ź and ż to the Latin alphabet. Dropping a diacritic usually produces a different word.", "kind": "character", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "262f831f9354677e0c4a6bf0ff620e23989c0d572f9158d9abad010f7339a8ee", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-norm", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.orto.diakrytyki", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Polish diacritics"}
{"body": "Address strangers, officials and older people with 'pan' or 'pani' plus the third person: 'Czy pan wie...?'. 'ty' is for friends, family, peers and children.", "kind": "pragmatics", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "4571815db20fd1d38ea526e1113524bde493e519a74fbd36bb1cf352c18511c0", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-original", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.prag.pan-ty", "themes": ["praca i biuro"], "title": "Choosing between pan/pani and ty"}
{"body": "'Przepraszam' both apologises and opens a request. Ask for repetition with 'Przepraszam, nie rozumiem' or 'Czy może pan powtórzyć?'.", "kind": "pragmatics", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "6c2dd75b8f2be1bba8406d5b7fb7d39cee6182dd3b5049db5252a9da2329600a", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-original", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.prag.przepraszam", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Apologising and asking for repetition"}
{"body": "The usual frame is 'Poproszę ...' plus the accusative: 'Poproszę kawę i wodę.' 'Chcę' alone can sound blunt.", "kind": "pragmatics", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "b59947956860a450e5f09000da824fe8f16686144294f3e37608cbaa715edfa0", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-original", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.prag.zamawianie", "themes": ["jedzenie"], "title": "Ordering politely in a restaurant"}
{"body": "'Smacznego' is said before eating, roughly 'enjoy your meal'; the reply is 'dziękuję' or 'smacznego'. Guests are often offered food more than once.", "kind": "culture", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "21c44cfb282eec870f2fbd4047d5e3708dcc08e3de5c72d8c1e0a45517a048ce", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-original", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.culture.smacznego", "themes": ["jedzenie"], "title": "Smacznego and mealtime conventions"}
{"body": "When a word is missing, describe it: 'to jest coś do ...', 'takie małe urządzenie'. This keeps a conversation going instead of stopping it.", "kind": "skill_strategy", "level": "A2", "provenance": {"content_hash": "ac6bf5a4bd563dea87028fa26bb5735788682ee25c9327ec94c88de00070ab46", "lifecycle": "verified", "origin_profile": "authored-original", "review_profile": "authored-verified", "risk_tier": 2}, "stable_key": "pl.strategy.omowienie", "themes": ["dom i codzienność"], "title": "Working around a missing word"}
